                         § 3. The structure of the graph L̃
   We regard L̃∞ as consisting of forks connected by “edge type” subgraphs. (The
definition is given below.) The purpose of this section is to demonstrate that there
are only finitely many possibilities for L̃∞ and to describe them all. We recall that
vertices of L̃∞ are marked by a tilde, and their images under F are denoted by
the same letters but without tilde. If a ∈ L, then we denote by a0 the set of all
a ∈ F −1 (a) such that F |ae is non-constant.
