   Now we begin the proof of theorem (0.2). Suppose the contrary: there exists a
four-sheeted mapping f with one dicritical component. Let Xe , X , F , Le, L be as in
Introduction. Since Xe and X are obtained from Ce2 and C 2 by blow-ups, the pairs
(e
 X; Le) and (X; L) are contractible to +1.
   It follows from [3, lemma 4.2] that the rami cation order of F along the dicritical
component is 1, 2, or 3. The rami cation of order 3 is impossible, see [3, remark
after 5.3 ]. If the rami cation order is 1 then F is an unbranched covering over C 2 .
This is also impossible. So, the only possible order of rami cation is 2.
   Let us denote the dicritical component of f by ge and put g = F (ge) and Le1 =
F 1(L). It follows from [3, lemma 4.2] that m(ge) = 1, n(ge) = 2. Resolving, if
necessary, the singularities of g at in nity, we can achieve that:
   (2a) ge has only one point of intersection with Le1 (see [3]). We denote this point
         by pe. Thus, Le1 is a branch of Le at ge.
  (2b) g \ L = p := F (pe), the intersection is transversal, and g is smooth at p.
We may assume that Xe and X are minimal possible under these conditions. So,
We may assume that Xe and X are minimal possible under these conditions. So,
