                     On four-sheeted polynomial
                  mappings of C2 . II. The general case


                                      A. V. Domrina

   Abstract. In this paper we prove that there are no four-sheeted polynomial map-
   pings of C2 into itself whose Jacobian is a non-zero constant.



                                    Introduction
       e
   Let C and C be two copies of the complex plane, and let f : C
         2        2                                                  e 2 → C2 be a
polynomial mapping whose Jacobian is a non-zero constant. The well-known Jacobi
conjecture states that f is polynomially invertible (see [1], [2].)
   The mapping f is polynomially invertible if and only if a generic point in C2
has exactly one pre-image. We define the topological degree of a mapping to be the
number of pre-images of a generic point. It is known that there are no polynomial
mappings of degree two or three whose Jacobian is a non-zero constant. The case
of degree two is elementary, and the case of degree three was considered in [3]. The
main result of this paper is as follows.
Theorem. Let f : C e 2 → C2 be a polynomial mapping of topological degree four.
Then the Jacobian of f cannot be a non-zero constant.
   This paper is a continuation of [4], where the case when f has one dicritical
component was proved to be impossible. Here we consider the remaining cases.
The proof of the theorem uses the notation and assertions in [4], § 1.
   The author is deeply grateful to A. G. Vitushkin and S. Yu. Orevkov for useful
discussions and for their help in preparing this paper.

